<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Négritude</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1930s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Negritude</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Négritude is a framework of critique and literary theory developed in the 1930s, mainly by French-speaking Black intellectuals and writers, to raise Black consciousness across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and its diaspora. Its founders included the Martinican poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aime-cesaire">Aimé Césaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-sedar-senghor">Léopold Sédar Senghor</a>, later the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, and Léon Damas of French Guiana. Césaire first used the word in its present sense in 1935 in L'Étudiant noir, a magazine he had started in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> with fellow students. Rejecting colonialism, racism and Eurocentrism, its writers drew on Marxism and Surrealism and celebrated African history and culture.</p></div>
